WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.583 --> 00:00:04.087
I think I converged on the idea of being a scientist fairly early on.

00:00:04.087 --> 00:00:07.507
We took a family trip to South Africa in 1998.

00:00:07.507 --> 00:00:09.009
We went to Kruger National Park

00:00:09.009 --> 00:00:10.635
and I recently found an old notebook

00:00:10.635 --> 00:00:12.554
where I was counting all of the antelopes

00:00:12.554 --> 00:00:14.806
that many people find quite boring.

00:00:14.806 --> 00:00:16.725
I was interested and engaged with the natural world.

00:00:16.725 --> 00:00:18.226
I was just really excited about it.

00:00:18.226 --> 00:00:22.439
I'm an ecologist of plants broadly, but more specifically

00:00:22.439 --> 00:00:23.648
I study savanna ecosystems.

00:00:23.648 --> 00:00:27.694
Savanna ecosystems are where trees and grass occur together.

00:00:31.156 --> 00:00:33.575
Most of the fires that burn on earth

00:00:33.575 --> 00:00:36.619
by a very large margin are savanna fires.

00:00:36.619 --> 00:00:39.039
They have a large contribution to the global carbon cycle.

00:00:39.039 --> 00:00:43.835
Fires take carbon from the biosphere or from essentially carbon that's stored in plants

00:00:43.918 --> 00:00:45.462
and move it into the atmosphere.

00:00:45.462 --> 00:00:50.050
And so if we're going to predict dynamics of the global carbon cycle accurately,

00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:54.012
including our fossil fuel contributions to atmospheric CO2,

00:00:54.012 --> 00:00:58.933
we also need to get a better handle on fire contributions to the global carbon cycle.

00:00:58.933 --> 00:01:01.144
And by far, the biggest contributor to that are savanna fires.

00:01:01.144 --> 00:01:04.689
I use a lot of different techniques in doing my science.

00:01:04.689 --> 00:01:06.691
We do a lot of remote sensing of the environment.

00:01:06.691 --> 00:01:09.944
Essentially, data that are derived either from airplanes or from satellites,

00:01:09.944 --> 00:01:11.404
increasingly from drones.

00:01:11.404 --> 00:01:15.408
You essentially fly a drone over a fixed plot,

00:01:15.408 --> 00:01:16.868
and then you start a fire and you watch the fire

00:01:16.868 --> 00:01:19.412
spread across the plot and you concurrently record

00:01:19.412 --> 00:01:22.665
wind speed, you record air temperature, you record air humidity.

00:01:22.665 --> 00:01:25.085
Filming the fire allows you to quantify

00:01:25.085 --> 00:01:27.253
how fire behavior changes in response

00:01:27.253 --> 00:01:29.380
to changing weather conditions.

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:32.008
We actually started in excess of 1100

00:01:32.008 --> 00:01:35.512
experimental fires across savannas in a variety of ecosystems.

00:01:35.512 --> 00:01:38.556
We're really interested in the question of what extinguishes fires.

00:01:38.556 --> 00:01:41.559
Something that stops a fire is a really important determinant

00:01:41.559 --> 00:01:46.064
of how big a fire gets and how much area it's going to burn.

00:01:46.064 --> 00:01:48.858
Most fire ecologists will tell you that they know why fires go out.

00:01:48.858 --> 00:01:50.944
It's for a variety of reasons, but why fires go out

00:01:50.944 --> 00:01:52.946
is not quantitatively very well understood

00:01:52.946 --> 00:01:54.697
by anybody in any ecosystem.

00:01:54.697 --> 00:01:57.575
The most surprising thing that we learned is that

00:01:57.575 --> 00:02:00.245
you can burn much wetter grass than you would think.

00:02:00.245 --> 00:02:02.497
There could be as much water as grass,

00:02:02.497 --> 00:02:04.916
and it still burns perfectly fine,

00:02:04.916 --> 00:02:07.418
which is not what people typically assume.

00:02:07.418 --> 00:02:08.503
And the way that that happens

00:02:08.503 --> 00:02:10.672
is that as long as you can get the fire started,

00:02:10.672 --> 00:02:13.424
the heat that that fire generates causes a lot of the water

00:02:13.424 --> 00:02:15.885
downstream from the fire front to evaporate.

00:02:15.885 --> 00:02:17.637
And then the fire becomes self-sustaining.

00:02:17.637 --> 00:02:20.682
That probably allows fires to get bigger than we currently think

00:02:20.682 --> 00:02:23.434
that they do under wetter circumstances.

00:02:23.434 --> 00:02:26.020
But it could also interact with things like emissions factors.

00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:29.274
Wet fires tend to be smokier fires that emit different

00:02:29.274 --> 00:02:30.400
atmospheric byproducts.

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:33.653
And so it could change some of the downstream estimates

00:02:33.653 --> 00:02:36.072
that we make of what the impacts of fires actually are.

00:02:36.072 --> 00:02:38.616
There's a lot to know and there's a lot to study.

00:02:38.616 --> 00:02:42.203
And what we know constitutes just a drop in the bucket of what

00:02:42.203 --> 00:02:43.496
we should or need to know.

